WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.634
♪♪

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:08.133
-A pizza slice
is not a triangle.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.801
It is a sector,

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:13.300
because the short side
is an arc.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.234
[ Laughter ]

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.067
I said I've come to terms
with the fact

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:25.033
that I'm never gonna say
"eureka!"

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:27.033
in a moment of discovery.

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:30.267
Then I had to clarify because --

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.367
and these are my words --
I said

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:37.067
some eagle-eyed viewers caught
that I had said "eureka"

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:38.734
in a previous "Corrections,"

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:40.200
and then a bunch of you piled on

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.634
and said it actually
wouldn't require eagle eyes,

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:45.868
because it would be
something that we heard.

00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:48.467
Unless the volume was down

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.534
and you were watching
the closed captioning.

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:51.801
Ya burnt.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.467
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:57.434
We said that
based on the regularity

00:00:57.434 --> 00:01:00.400
with which he commits crimes,
it would not be surprising

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.300
if on the day he announced
he was running for president,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.267
Donald Trump shoplifted a pair
of earrings from Claire's.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:11.767
We then zoomed in,
and in his hand,

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.033
a bunch of you said

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.334
that's actually a 20-pack
of earrings from Claire's.

00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:19.634
So he didn't just steal one
pair of earrings, he stole 20.

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:24.467
But what if, in the world
we had built,

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:27.000
he paid for 19...

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:30.901
...and then stole one?

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:36.067
Why do you all have to jump
to the worst-case scenario

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.234
with this man?

00:01:37.234 --> 00:01:40.234
Of whom I'm not a fan!

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:45.901
But this "guilty until proven
innocent" has to stop.

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.267
Shame on you.

00:01:51.834 --> 00:01:53.467
You guys, a few years back,

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:55.501
Randy Johnson killed a burn.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:58.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.634
He killed a bird.
We all saw it.

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:05.501
We all know we were
supposed to feel bad.

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:07.501
We loved it.

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:10.334
He killed a bird.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:13.501
The bird was, I discovered,'
a mourning dove,

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:17.334
spelled M-O-U-R-N.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:20.334
I then made the observation

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:24.334
that if a bird
already in mourning

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:27.300
was hit by a baseball

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.467
and exploded,

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:33.634
that would be like a hearse

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:38.133
driving off
the side of a cliff.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:40.000
A lot to unpack here.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:42.667
Let's begin.
Alright.

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:44.767
I was told by a lot of you
the analogy doesn't work

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:46.601
because a hearse
going off a cliff

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:48.367
would only take the life
of the hearse driver,

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.701
who is not in mourning.

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.367
He's just working.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.501
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.133 --> 00:03:00.200
Like, he has no...
He's just a guy doing his job.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.367
Like, that's just freight.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:09.901
Nothing more, nothing less.

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:11.968
He just punched in,

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.601
drove that coffin
from there to there,

00:03:14.601 --> 00:03:15.934
punched out.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:21.000
With that said, I'm sure
professional hearse drivers

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.033
would tell you you want
to maintain, like, stoicism.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:28.901
You want to feel --
you want to project sadness.

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:31.033
You don't want to just
be like, "Load 'em up!"

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:32.534
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:34.934
"My third one today!

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.601
Must be something going around."

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:40.267
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:42.267
"Hey, lady, your husband
like Night Ranger?

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:44.634
Might blast some tunes."

00:03:44.634 --> 00:03:46.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:49.000
"On the way to the G-yard."

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.367 --> 00:04:00.601
Also, I was told another error

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:05.067
in that you can't drive off
the side of a cliff.

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:09.467
You can only drive off
the top of a cliff

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.267
and then you plummet
down the side.

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.067
With that said, if everybody --

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:17.033
anybody ever tells
any of you jackals

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.033
they lost a loved one

00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:22.200
when their car went over
the side of a cliff,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.868
let it go.

00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:25.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:32.033
We were talking about doves
and pigeons,

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:34.901
and we talked
about pigeons cooing.

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:37.367
I thought pigeons
and doves sounded the same.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.234
I was told
they're very different.

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.467
Here's the sound
of a pigeon.

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:43.801
[ Pigeon cooing ]

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:46.300
Here's the sound of a dove.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.234
[ Dove cooing ]

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:51.067
And here is the sound

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.567
of a white-winged dove.

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:55.567
[ "The Edge of 17" plays ]
-♪ Ooh, ooh ooh ♪

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:57.434
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:05.300
Any Nicks fans in the house?

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:14.868
We're coming up on Emmy season,

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:17.534
which means a lot of people
in the biz

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:21.634
are coming down
with Emmy fever.

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.267
Emmy fever, it should be noted,

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:26.234
CAN be treated with ivermectin.

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:28.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.234 --> 00:05:31.234
A couple of -- couple of squirts
from the old tube.

00:05:31.234 --> 00:05:34.234
[ Laughing ]

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:38.567
Hey, I've been making clear

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:44.067
that the Specjackular is not
and has never been

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:46.701
an episode of "Corrections."

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.501
It is its own thing.

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:52.501
One might even say you would
categorize it differently.

00:05:54.234 --> 00:05:56.100
But then a bunch of you said,

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.734
"Hey, if that's the case,

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:02.434
why is it listed as episode 50?"

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:04.868
Shut the [bleep] up.

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:06.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.901
What are you doing?

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:13.334
Shut the [bleep] up.

00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:17.901
[ Scoffs ]

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:22.167
Oh, we asked you guys
to help us

00:06:22.167 --> 00:06:24.634
pick our Emmys submission
for "Corrections,"

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:26.133
and it's been pretty resounding.

00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:27.300
There are two.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.033
I would say the front-runner
is episode 66.

00:06:30.033 --> 00:06:32.267
That's our Christmas episode.

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:35.367
Also, episode 69.
We're gonna put those up.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:37.434
Let's put those up
on Sea Captain Polls

00:06:37.434 --> 00:06:38.901
as our two options.

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:41.367
I'd like you to watch
them both again.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.701
I genuinely mean this,

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.133
'cause I think
66 is a lot of fun.

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:48.934
It seems maybe more dependent
on previous episodes.

00:06:48.934 --> 00:06:51.167
69 might be a better standalone.
I don't know.

00:06:51.167 --> 00:06:52.601
I'm gonna let you guys decide.

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:55.267
But this is a big deal.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:59.334
Take it seriously because
if everything breaks our way,

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:02.434
the episode you choose

00:07:02.434 --> 00:07:05.667
will have the honor
of losing to Carpool Karaoke.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:11.801
I know -- I think I know
the difference,

00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:14.467
which I think works against us.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:19.000
Any episode of Carpool Karaoke
is good.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Any episode of "Corrections"
is good

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.501
if you've watched
every other episode.

00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:27.200
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.133
We're asking for a lot of time.

00:07:34.834 --> 00:07:36.834
I like when someone writes
a comment

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:39.167
that makes it very clear this is
their first "Corrections,"

00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:40.701
that they've stumbled upon it,

00:07:40.701 --> 00:07:42.200
maybe the algorithm
pushed it their way,

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.300
and they say something
that makes it very clear

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.501
they haven't watched
this before.

00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:49.133
Someone wrote,

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:52.400
"This has to be the worst
studio audience in TV."

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.400
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.467
Look, I get it -- if you're
watching this cold, you might --

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:03.968
This looks the same way
our show looks,

00:08:03.968 --> 00:08:07.234
so you might think,
"Wow, this is just a --

00:08:07.234 --> 00:08:10.067
this is just a dead audience."

00:08:10.067 --> 00:08:12.567
I get that.

00:08:12.567 --> 00:08:16.234
But you don't think
I'd wear it at all?

00:08:16.234 --> 00:08:18.167
Like, I'm pretty upbeat
during Correc-- I mean,

00:08:18.167 --> 00:08:20.400
if this was
a full audience

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.968
making the sounds they've made,
I'd just be like,

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:24.934
"Aw, [bleep]"

00:08:24.934 --> 00:08:26.801
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.868 --> 00:08:30.234
[ Sniffles ]

00:08:35.567 --> 00:08:39.100
Now, there are other people who
think there are too many laughs,

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:41.300
and that maybe
we're sweetening it a little,

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.801
like the jackal who wrote,

00:08:42.801 --> 00:08:46.467
"Does the staff get paid extra
for laughing at 'Corrections'?"

00:08:46.467 --> 00:08:49.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:50.601
They do. Per laugh.

00:08:50.601 --> 00:08:52.901
I pay them each per laugh.

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:55.033
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:59.434
So it's like $9 that I split
among most everybody.

00:08:59.434 --> 00:09:01.100
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:03.767
But Buck just bought
his second yacht.

00:09:03.767 --> 00:09:06.767
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:09:06.767 --> 00:09:08.767
Got a backup yacht.

00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:10.701
[ Laughter ]

00:09:14.033 --> 00:09:15.701
[ Sighing ] Having --

00:09:15.701 --> 00:09:17.200
I've been having
a little bit of fun

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.400
mispronouncing the name
of a famous Greek mathematician.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.868
I've said "Arch-i-meds."

00:09:25.868 --> 00:09:28.167
I've said "Archie-meads."

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:31.834
I know, by the way.

00:09:31.834 --> 00:09:33.968
I know the correct pronunciation

00:09:33.968 --> 00:09:35.100
is Archime--

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.934
-Deez nuts!

00:09:36.934 --> 00:09:39.300
[ Laughter ]

00:09:42.434 --> 00:09:44.267
-Eureka!

00:09:44.267 --> 00:09:46.133
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.567
Alright, we were talking
about Flaco the owl

00:09:55.567 --> 00:09:59.334
and how nobody needs to be
worried about Flaco the owl

00:09:59.334 --> 00:10:01.133
not finding food
'cause he eats rats

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:03.934
and he's in Central Park,

00:10:03.934 --> 00:10:07.167
and an owl who eats rats
starving in Central Park

00:10:07.167 --> 00:10:10.434
would be like a toddler starving
in a chicken nugget factory.

00:10:10.434 --> 00:10:11.934
A bunch of you said the nuggets
would be frozen,

00:10:11.934 --> 00:10:15.434
so the kids at the factory
would starve.

00:10:15.434 --> 00:10:18.334
And then a bunch of you took
the other side of the argument,

00:10:18.334 --> 00:10:20.667
my initial position,

00:10:20.667 --> 00:10:24.901
that kids in a chicken nugget
factory would be fine.

00:10:24.901 --> 00:10:26.400
Including someone
who wrote,

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:27.734
"Nuggets in
a chicken nugget factory

00:10:27.734 --> 00:10:29.133
would not all be frozen.

00:10:29.133 --> 00:10:31.334
Freezing them is one of
the last steps before shipping.

00:10:31.334 --> 00:10:35.334
First, the chicken is ground up
and mixed with chicken skins,

00:10:35.334 --> 00:10:40.133
then combined with a beige paste
to make a chicken-flavored goo

00:10:40.133 --> 00:10:42.634
that is poured
into nugget-shaped molds.

00:10:42.634 --> 00:10:44.200
They are then fried in batter."

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.400
I don't know what
that person's job is.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.634
[ Laughter ]

00:10:49.634 --> 00:10:53.467
But I'm guessing it's not head
of marketing for Tyson Chicken.

00:10:53.467 --> 00:10:56.133
[ Laughter ]

00:10:56.133 --> 00:10:57.868
"For the next round
of commercials,

00:10:57.868 --> 00:11:01.601
I think we got to really
lean into beige paste."

00:11:01.601 --> 00:11:03.334
[ Laughter ]

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.334
Speaking of the owl,

00:11:11.334 --> 00:11:14.634
I mentioned how one of our
crew members, Kenny Coyle,

00:11:14.634 --> 00:11:17.300
has been telling everybody
he could catch the owl.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.634
[ Laughter ]

00:11:19.634 --> 00:11:22.300
I said a few lines as Kenny,

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.934
and then some people wrote
and said,

00:11:26.934 --> 00:11:28.434
"Based on how he's talking,

00:11:28.434 --> 00:11:30.767
is Kenny Shoemaker's kid?"

00:11:30.767 --> 00:11:33.434
[ Laughter ]

00:11:33.434 --> 00:11:39.067
And I want to apologize
for not making this clear.

00:11:39.067 --> 00:11:41.400
Almost everyone who works here

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.901
is one of Shoemaker's kids.

00:11:43.901 --> 00:11:45.968
[ Laughter ]

00:11:45.968 --> 00:11:49.801
They just keep coming out
of the woodwork.

00:11:49.801 --> 00:11:51.267
[ Laughing ]

00:11:53.434 --> 00:11:54.934
And when they do,

00:11:54.934 --> 00:11:58.601
they're what Shoemaker
classifies as must-hires.

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:00.667
[ Laughter ]

00:12:00.667 --> 00:12:01.868
Don't get me wrong,

00:12:01.868 --> 00:12:04.868
he's not happy about it.

00:12:04.868 --> 00:12:05.934
The amount...

00:12:05.934 --> 00:12:08.267
[ Laughing ]

00:12:08.267 --> 00:12:11.067
The amount of times he comes,
he comes into my office

00:12:11.067 --> 00:12:13.133
and just plops down
on the couch and is like,

00:12:13.133 --> 00:12:15.300
"There's another one
at the page desk."

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.133
[ Laughter ]

00:12:18.133 --> 00:12:21.133
He's so -- He's so mad
he did 23andMe.

00:12:21.133 --> 00:12:23.267
[ Laughter ]

00:12:23.267 --> 00:12:25.100
He's so mad,
and it's such a burn on him,

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:27.634
'cause he did it
because none of us believed

00:12:27.634 --> 00:12:30.133
he descended
from the Medicis.

00:12:30.133 --> 00:12:31.767
[ Laughter ]

00:12:33.701 --> 00:12:35.868
Oh, my God.

00:12:35.868 --> 00:12:38.834
He wanted to prove
he was Italian royalty.

00:12:38.834 --> 00:12:40.834
[ Laughter ]

00:12:40.834 --> 00:12:44.334
And instead, once a week,
someone from reception calls up

00:12:44.334 --> 00:12:47.501
and says, "Mr. Shoemaker,
there's another genetic match."

00:12:47.501 --> 00:12:49.234
[ Laughter ]

00:12:53.567 --> 00:12:55.067
Someone said

00:12:55.067 --> 00:12:59.501
I said people are jealous
of my Mac Tonight toys.

00:13:00.968 --> 00:13:03.267
And someone said
they wouldn't be jealous --

00:13:03.267 --> 00:13:05.267
they would be envious.

00:13:06.267 --> 00:13:07.934
So you're telling me...

00:13:07.934 --> 00:13:10.501
someone wouldn't be jealous...

00:13:10.501 --> 00:13:12.167
of this?

00:13:12.167 --> 00:13:14.367
[ Laughter ]

00:13:20.367 --> 00:13:24.033
Every time you think
it can't get [bleep] worse...

00:13:24.033 --> 00:13:26.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:32.167 --> 00:13:36.834
A lot of -- We talked about
people dying of old age,

00:13:36.834 --> 00:13:41.167
and then someone said, no,
people don't die of old age.

00:13:41.167 --> 00:13:43.834
What happens is their body just
breaks down as they get older

00:13:43.834 --> 00:13:46.701
and they get more
susceptible to illness.

00:13:46.701 --> 00:13:50.033
And then a lot of you said,
"No, no, no,

00:13:50.033 --> 00:13:51.367
people can die of old age."

00:13:51.367 --> 00:13:54.734
And one person said,
to prove this point,

00:13:54.734 --> 00:13:56.601
they said the Queen's
death certificate

00:13:56.601 --> 00:13:58.934
said she had died of old age.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.634
But come on.

00:14:02.634 --> 00:14:04.300
They weren't going
to put "auto-affix"--

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:05.801
[ Laughing ]

00:14:05.801 --> 00:14:07.801
[ Laughter ]

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.667
Ah, well.

